"""Slides (1920x1080 html) and narration text of the weekly progress video, 9 Nov 2026. Numbers come from results/weekly_tables.json (src/162_weekly_tables.py). usage (from video/weekly1): python make_slides.py ; render.sh ; tts_kokoro.py audio ; build.sh audio weekly1.mp4"""
import json
T = json.load(open('../../results/weekly_tables.json')); D = T['doses']; ms = T['mert_seeds']
BG, FG, MUT, ACC, ACC2, LINE = '#1B2430', '#F6F4EE', '#C9D1DB', '#F2A65A', '#7FB7BE', '#3A4656'
CSS = f"""html,body{{margin:0;background:#000}}section{{box-sizing:border-box;width:1920px;height:1080px;overflow:hidden;background:{BG};color:{FG};font-family:'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;padding:90px 120px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:34px}}
h1{{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:64px;margin:0;font-weight:600;line-height:1.12}}h2{{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:58px;margin:0;font-weight:600;line-height:1.12}}
p,li{{font-size:34px;line-height:1.38;margin:0;color:{MUT}}}ul{{margin:0;padding-left:44px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px}}
.k{{font-size:24px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;color:{ACC}}}b{{color:{FG}}}.s{{font-size:26px;color:#98A4B3}}
.row{{display:flex;gap:70px}}.col{{flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:22px}}
table{{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:30px;color:{MUT}}}th,td{{padding:10px 24px;border-bottom:1px solid {LINE};text-align:left}}th{{color:{FG}}}"""
def slide(n, body, k='', text=''):
    open(f'frames/{n:02d}.html', 'w').write(f'<!doctype html><meta charset=utf-8><style>{CSS}</style><section>' + (f'<div class=k>{k}</div>' if k else '') + body + '</section>')
    open(f'script/{n:02d}.txt', 'w').write(text.strip() + '\n')
def chart():
    W, H, X0, Y0 = 1000, 560, 90, 30; xs = [float(c[1:]) for c in D]
    px = lambda x: X0 + x * (W - X0 - 20); py = lambda y: Y0 + (H - Y0 - 70) * (1 - y / 5)
    s = f'<svg width="{W}" height="{H}" viewBox="0 0 {W} {H}" font-family="Helvetica Neue,Arial" font-size="24">'
    for v in range(6): s += f'<line x1="{X0}" x2="{W-20}" y1="{py(v)}" y2="{py(v)}" stroke="{LINE}"/><text x="{X0-14}" y="{py(v)+8}" fill="{MUT}" text-anchor="end">{v}</text>'
    for x in (0, .2, .4, .6, .8, 1): s += f'<text x="{px(x)}" y="{H-36}" fill="{MUT}" text-anchor="middle">{x:g}</text>'
    s += f'<text x="{(X0+W)/2}" y="{H-4}" fill="{MUT}" text-anchor="middle">dose a (0 = 1960s prototype, 1 = 2010s prototype)</text>'
    for m, col in (('adapter', ACC), ('control', ACC2)):
        pts = ' '.join(f'{px(x):.1f},{py(ms[m][c]["mean"]):.1f}' for x, c in zip(xs, D)); s += f'<polyline points="{pts}" fill="none" stroke="{col}" stroke-width="5"/>'
        for x, c in zip(xs, D): s += f'<circle cx="{px(x):.1f}" cy="{py(ms[m][c]["mean"]):.1f}" r="7" fill="{col}"/>'
    s += f'<text x="{px(.62)}" y="{py(4.9)}" fill="{ACC}">adapter</text><text x="{px(.62)}" y="{py(4.45)}" fill="{ACC2}">control</text></svg>'
    return s
slide(1, f"""<div style="flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;gap:34px"><div class=k>Weekly progress · 9 November 2026</div>
<h1 style="font-size:88px">Era and market as two controls for cover generation</h1><p>One song, regenerated as the point (release era, release market) moves.</p></div>
<div class=s>James Christian · Liang-Yu Hsia · Jefferson Sy Dionisio</div>""",
text="Weekly progress report. Our goal is a control plane for cover generation with two axes: the release era of the recording, and its release market. Keep one song fixed, move the point on the plane, and the cover changes with it. For example, a recent American song regenerated as if it came from Brazil in two thousand ten. This week we measured how well each axis can be controlled, and how the two combine.")
slide(2, f"""<h2>Two axes, two different carriers</h2><div class=row><div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">Era axis</p><ul><li>A conditioner on a frozen generator, with a <b>continuous dose</b> a from the 1960s prototype to the 2010s prototype.</li>
<li>The melody of the original is kept through its chroma.</li></ul></div><div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">Market axis</p><ul><li>The market is <b>carried by the sung language</b>: translated lyrics, with the vocal replaced by a target-language voice.</li>
<li>A <b>language dose</b> is set by the share of code-switched lyrics.</li></ul></div></div>
<p>Read-outs are existing metrics only; no new metric is proposed.</p>""", 'Setup',
"The era axis is a conditioner on a frozen generator, with a continuous dose from zero, the nineteen sixties prototype, to one, the twenty tens prototype. The melody of the original is kept through its chroma. The market axis is carried by the sung language. We translate the lyrics, replace the vocal with a target language voice, and set a language dose by the share of code switched lyrics. All read outs are existing metrics. We propose no new metric.")
slide(3, f"""<h2>Era dose response</h2><div class=row><div>{chart()}</div><div class=col><ul><li>Expected decade of the generated accompaniment, MERT probe, mean of <b>three training seeds</b>.</li>
<li>Monotone for every run; the two models meet at both end points.</li><li>An independent encoder, Discogs-EffNet, orders the doses the same way.</li><li>Chroma similarity to the source stays between .81 and .84.</li></ul></div></div>""", 'Era axis',
"This is the era dose response. The vertical axis is the expected decade index of the generated accompaniment, from zero for the nineteen sixties to five for the twenty tens, measured with the MERT probe and averaged over three training seeds. Both models are monotone in every run, and they meet at both end points. An independent encoder, Discogs EffNet, orders the doses the same way, and the chroma similarity to the source stays between point eight one and point eight four.")
a5, c5 = ms['adapter']['a0.5']['mean'], ms['control']['a0.5']['mean']
slide(4, f"""<h2>The adapter reaches the decade at a lower dose</h2><div class=row><div class=col><table><tr><th>dose</th><th>adapter</th><th>control</th></tr>{''.join(f'<tr><td>{c[1:]}</td><td>{ms["adapter"][c]["mean"]:.2f}</td><td>{ms["control"][c]["mean"]:.2f}</td></tr>' for c in ('a0.3','a0.4','a0.5','a0.6','a0.7'))}</table></div>
<div class=col><ul><li>Between doses .3 and .7 the adapter is ahead on MERT and EffNet in <b>all three training seeds</b>.</li><li>FAD with sample-size extrapolation does <b>not</b> rank the two consistently across seeds.</li>
<li>So the claim is a lower dose for the same decade, not more realistic audio.</li></ul></div></div>""", 'Adapter versus control',
f"Compared with a control conditioner that uses the same route without the adapter bias, the adapter reaches the target decade at a lower dose. At dose point five the mean decade index is {a5:.2f} for the adapter and {c5:.2f} for the control. Between doses point three and point seven, the adapter is ahead on MERT and on EffNet in all three training seeds. But the distribution level metric, FAD with sample size extrapolation, does not rank the two models consistently across seeds. So our claim is that the adapter reaches the decade at a lower dose, not that it generates more realistic audio of the decade.")
slide(5, f"""<h2>Market axis and the combined grid</h2><div class=row><div class=col><ul><li>Erasing the language direction from the vocal probe <b>removes</b> the market move.</li>
<li>Language share moves the mix-level language probability by <b>+.71 to +.78</b>.</li><li>Vocal market probe: <b>+.28</b> Spanish, <b>+.32</b> German.</li></ul></div>
<div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">5 × 5 grid, es and de, 16 songs, 3 seeds</p><ul><li>Era effect <b>+.62 to +1.00</b> expected decades at every language share.</li><li>Language effect <b>+.71 to +.78</b> at every era dose.</li><li>Confidence intervals exclude zero.</li></ul></div></div>""", 'Market axis · both axes',
"For the market axis, erasing the language direction from the vocal probe removes the market move, so the sung language is the carrier. The language share moves the language probability of the mix by point seven one to point seven eight. The vocal market probe moves by point two eight for Spanish and point three two for German. In the combined grid, five era doses by five language shares, for Spanish and German, sixteen songs and three seeds, the era effect is point six two to one point zero zero expected decades at every language share, and the language effect is point seven one to point seven eight at every era dose. The confidence intervals exclude zero.")
slide(6, f"""<h2>How quality is assessed and compared</h2><div class=row><div class=col><ul><li><b>Controllability:</b> probe index and gain, R-Precision and multimodal distance, dose monotonicity.</li><li><b>Distribution:</b> FAD per cell, extrapolated to infinite sample size.</li>
<li><b>Variety:</b> Diversity and MultiModality.</li><li><b>Content:</b> song identification, chroma similarity.</li></ul></div>
<div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">Compared with previous work</p><ul><li>Baselines re-run on the <b>same 24 songs</b>: text-only generation, melody plus text prompts, a melody adapter, an era LoRA, training-free steering, best-of-N.</li><li>Numbers of other papers are <b>not mixed in</b>.</li></ul></div></div>""", 'Evaluation',
"On quality assessment. We use existing metrics only. For controllability: probe index and gain, R precision and multimodal distance in embedding space, adapted from the text to motion literature, and dose monotonicity. For distribution quality: FAD per cell against real clips of the decade, extrapolated to infinite sample size. For variety: diversity and multimodality. For content: song identification and chroma similarity. For comparison with previous work, we re run the available baselines on the same twenty four songs, and we do not mix numbers from other papers into our tables, since data, embeddings and references differ.")
slide(7, f"""<h2>What the lecture on MIDI-to-audio adds</h2><ul><li><b>Relevant:</b> symbolic melody as the condition instead of chroma, as in recent work that conditions a pop generator on transcribed MIDI.</li>
<li><b>Relevant:</b> the monophonic note-to-audio problem is singing voice synthesis, which fits the target-language vocal of the market axis.</li><li><b>Not relevant:</b> classical synthesizer types and single-note neural models.</li></ul>
<p>Next: verify the cited papers, then test transcribed MIDI against chroma on the same songs and read-outs.</p>""", 'Course material',
"The lecture on MIDI to audio generation helps in two places. First, a symbolic melody as the condition instead of chroma, as in recent work that conditions a pop generator on transcribed MIDI. Second, the monophonic note to audio problem is singing voice synthesis, which fits the target language vocal of the market axis. The classical synthesizer types and single note neural models are not relevant. Next we verify the cited papers, then test transcribed MIDI against chroma on the same songs and read outs.")
slide(8, f"""<h2>Limits and next steps</h2><div class=row><div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">Limits</p><ul><li>24 validation songs; one validation split.</li><li>Probes are proxies trained on real clips.</li><li>FAD does not separate the two models.</li><li><b>No listening test</b>: no claim about how the covers sound to people.</li></ul></div>
<div class=col><p class=k style="color:{ACC2}">Next</p><ul><li>Page, report and demo clips updated; vocal market demos stay internal.</li><li>MIDI melody condition; accompaniment-generation baseline.</li><li>Country metadata of real cover pairs.</li></ul></div></div>
<p class=s>Code, results and demo clips: goog-msft-fb-nflx-nvda-aapl.github.io/musicgen-steering-eval/era_market</p>""", 'Summary',
"The limits are clear. Twenty four validation songs and one validation split. The probes are proxies trained on real clips. FAD does not separate the two models. And we ran no listening test, so we make no claim about how the covers sound to people. Next steps are the MIDI melody condition, an accompaniment generation baseline, and country metadata for real cover pairs. The code, results and demo clips are on our project page. Thank you.")
